e first  thing they point out too we had we had a  haier originally but because we're a new  organization because we don't have a  large email list we wanted the barrier  of entry to be a little bit lower so we  have $5,000 right now about 40 hours of  lobbying and the thing is that we're  gonna do a lot of the backend research  so when you come up when you start a  lobbying campaign and there's a lot of  work around strategy researching the  issue identifying targets on the hill  we're gonna do a lot of that so that the  people that we hire will be able to go  directly to the hill and use that that  time to meet one-on-one with legislators  and their staff so let's think through  sort of in total when we think about the  amount of money that might be raised on  your platform for individual lobbying  efforts hypothetically if all of that  money were pooled together and used to  try to change the system of lobbying and  campaign finance that we have might it  have a bigger impact it's an interesting  question but I think we might be  thinking a little bit too big I started  the platform to help people there's a  woman who started a campaign who's a  female veteran and there's a bill in in  Congress that would help force the VA to  look after female veterans because it's  just when such a male-dominated system  so that type of effort could pass a bill  like that 5,000 10,000 because it has  bipartisan support and just needs a push  to the end we lobby for the Institute of  Peace which is an organization that  tries to mitigate unless environment  conflicts are in the world and we spent  about 40 hours and we were able to  increase their budget by three million  dollars so do I think that this is a  platform that's going to change our  democracy and fix every problem that we  have I don't know I hope it's a useful  tool but it's one of many many things  that need to happen but I think that  we're gonna be very successful by  helping these these issues and these  these 