 give a dime to any political candidates  we didn't you know walk into the  Congress with a bag of money we emailed  him and he used a lot of his personal  connections  when he worked on the hill and we just  asked for 30 minutes of people's time  and we went in and we went in with a  good argument and we said you know this  is why the Institute of Peace needs to  be funded at a higher level the war in  Iraq is winding down and when it does  there gonna be a lot of conflicts  between the groups that came together to  fight Isis or ISIL and we need somebody  to be there to talk to those groups and  have a peace mediation conflict  resolution and it worked and we were  able to get three million dollars pretty  much on a three thousand dollar effort  so that they could increase the programs  that they were doing in Iraq and  Afghanistan so to answer your question  if sorry if I was long-winded  it's about accessing the people who need  to you need to reach and it's about  going in with a good argument and then  having an appropriate follow-up for when  the bill that you're concerned about is  in the legislative process to reach out  to those people and say listen this is  how I want you to vote this is I need  you to co-sponsor and kind of that's how  you can work the system so 3,000 and  lobbying spend for three million in  funds seems like is that a tenth out  10,000 percent return I'm not sure I  didn't exactly run the numbers but it  seems like it might be a 10,000 percent  return do you have data about what the  return is on lobbying investment either  across the entire lobbying space within  particular industries or you know any  data you might have about that it's all  it's not our data but it's the sunlight  foundation did a study and they found  and I'll give three examples when oil  companies lobbied for a subsidy that  would benefit them they found a 5,000  percent return on their investment when  corporations banded together and lobbied  for a tax holiday of corporate tax  holiday and I think the year was 2003  2004 they found a twenty two thousand  percent return on their investment and  when pharmaceutical companies lobbied  for the provision in Medicare where they  couldn't compete the drug prices and I'm  a little iffy on exactly what they  before but it was about you know  competing for drug prices 76,000 percent  return on their investment and so we're  hoping that we can have that kind of are  aligned for the people and I'll just  really quick I used to work in foreign  affairs foreign aid and we lobbied for  an increased budget in foreign aid and  you know I gave $20 to Save the Children  a wonderful organization that does a lot  of good work around the world and here  in the US and then when we lobbied for  them we were able to get bumps of two  million four million so individuals can  do a great service by donating but a lot  of the change really does come from  government whether it's a policy whether  it's funding the return on investment of  the lobbying a lobbyist is staggering  how does your organization determine  what a good project is in other words  people could disagree about what  priorities should be or about what  really deserves attention or lobbying  funds spent on it so what what sort of  the infrastructure to determine what is  good enough well that's a great question  and in one way we struggled with we  don't want to drive policy we want to be  a tool a resource to connect people  outside of the Beltway to people who  have influence in government so that  they can have a stronger voice and so  what we decided to do is just have a set  of founding principles that every  lobbying campaign must meet and if it  meets those founding principles we will  host it on a platform even if it's  something that we don't necessarily  support a hundred percent but everything  within those founding principles will  will be hosted on a platform and it's  pretty simple it's it has to be for the  common good 